WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:04.799
What's the difference between Docker and virtual machines, or VMs?

00:00:04.830 --> 00:00:18.870
Well, we're going to 1) define these two technologies then 2) describe how they work 3) give a bit of guidance on which workloads fit best for each technology.

00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:27.420
Now, both of these technologies address something in common and that thing in common is called virtualization.

00:00:28.750 --> 00:00:36.429
And what I mean by virtualization is a process where software is used to create an abstraction layer.

00:00:36.760 --> 00:00:45.057
So for VMs, that abstraction layer, or that abstraction software, is called a hypervisor.

00:00:47.470 --> 00:00:55.420
And a hypervisor is, simply put, something that helps a virtual machine emulate a physical computer.

00:00:55.450 --> 00:01:01.099
So underneath the hypervisor here we have some hardware.

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:10.889
And the hypervisor manages the allocation of resources between different virtual machines on that single physical host.

00:01:10.900 --> 00:01:15.090
So up here we have a number of VMs.

00:01:16.410 --> 00:01:22.440
Now each VM runs its own operating system and it has its own virtual hardware.

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:25.709
So like virtual CPU, virtual storage, that sort of thing.

00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:28.340
Now, what about Docker?

00:01:28.340 --> 00:01:33.709
Well, Docker is an open source platform that uses containerization technology.

00:01:33.740 --> 00:01:44.702
It allows developers to package applications and their dependencies into lightweight, portable containers.

00:01:45.550 --> 00:01:58.041
Instead of virtualizing the underlying hardware like this hypervisor is doing for the VMs, Docker containers virtualize the operating system.

00:01:58.300 --> 00:02:05.390
So each individual container contains only the application and its libraries and dependencies.

00:02:05.410 --> 00:02:11.020
So let's break down the main components of both solutions, and we'll start with Docker.

00:02:11.830 --> 00:02:17.379
So the first component I want to tell you about is called the Docker engine.

00:02:18.530 --> 00:02:23.830
Now, this is the core software that's responsible for managing the lifecycle of Docker containers.

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:30.019
So we're talking about things like providing the infrastructure for creating, running and orchestrating the containers.

00:02:30.380 --> 00:02:37.159
And the Docker engine interacts with the host kernel to allocate resources and enforce isolation between containers.

00:02:37.160 --> 00:02:47.419
And that's done through two things primarily; there's something called cgroups or control groups, and they allocate resources among the processes.

00:02:47.720 --> 00:02:57.342
And then there's something else called namespaces, and namespaces restrict a container's access and visibility to other resources on the system.

00:02:57.342 --> 00:03:00.919
And that ensures that each container has its own isolated environment.

00:03:02.170 --> 00:03:07.321
Now there's also Docker images.

00:03:09.010 --> 00:03:17.620
And Docker images are lightweight, standalone and executable packages that include everything you need to run a piece of software.

00:03:17.650 --> 00:03:24.340
So we're talking about the code for the software, the runtime, the system tools, the libraries and any settings that we need as well.

00:03:24.940 --> 00:03:37.120
And Docker images are built using Docker files, which are very simple documents which provide instructions for creating the image.

00:03:38.090 --> 00:03:45.517
And then there's also the Docker containers themselves.

00:03:46.420 --> 00:03:53.710
And these are the instances of the images that run in the Docker engine.

00:03:54.160 --> 00:04:00.182
Each container is an isolated and self-sufficient environment that includes only the necessary components

00:04:00.182 --> 00:04:05.529
for running a specific application and can be quickly started, stopped and restarted.

00:04:06.220 --> 00:04:14.742
Now, for VMs, we already know the hypervisor is the software responsible for creating, managing and running these virtual machines.

00:04:14.742 --> 00:04:24.574
And hypervisors come in two types: so we have a Type 1 hypervisor, that's also known as a bare metal hypervisor.

00:04:25.030 --> 00:04:29.242
And that runs directly on the host hardware, so right on that computer there.

00:04:29.290 --> 00:04:40.660
There is also a Type 2, and Type 2 is considered a hosted hypervisor, and that's where the hypervisor runs on top of an existing operating system.

00:04:41.860 --> 00:04:46.897
Now, the other components we should be concerned about, well, there's the virtual hardware.

00:04:47.390 --> 00:04:51.499
And this refers to the emulated components of a virtual machine.

00:04:51.830 --> 00:04:58.519
So I'm talking about things like a virtual CPU, virtual memory, virtual storage, virtual network interfaces.

00:04:58.520 --> 00:05:04.069
And these components are presented to the guest operating system as if they were real hardware.

00:05:04.490 --> 00:05:10.070
The hypervisor is responsible for managing and allocating these virtual resources to each VM.

00:05:10.700 --> 00:05:16.670
And yeah, speaking of guest OS, that's another core part of all of this, the guest operating system.

00:05:17.030 --> 00:05:20.749
Those are the individual operating systems that run inside each virtual machine.

00:05:20.750 --> 00:05:27.560
And each VM can have its own guest OS, which may differ from the host OS and other VMs on the same host.

00:05:27.830 --> 00:05:33.769
And that allows users to run multiple operating systems and applications on the same physical machine.

00:05:35.220 --> 00:05:38.472
Now, when to pick one over the other?

00:05:38.970 --> 00:05:46.289
While the choice isn't always black and white, let's start with some common use cases for VMs.

00:05:47.010 --> 00:05:48.930
When would you use a virtual machine?

00:05:48.930 --> 00:05:51.089
And I can think of three right away.

00:05:51.750 --> 00:05:57.559
And number 1, really, the obvious one, is the diverse operating systems that I just mentioned.

00:05:58.380 --> 00:06:05.939
VMs let you run different operating systems, so we could have a VM running Windows, another VM running Linux, all on this same piece of hardware.

00:06:05.940 --> 00:06:10.156
And that's handy when you're testing applications across multiple platforms, for example.

00:06:10.156 --> 00:06:15.089
A second big advantage is isolation.

00:06:16.090 --> 00:06:19.759
Now both solutions offer levels of isolation, but with the VMs,

00:06:19.759 --> 00:06:27.399
isolation is it's pretty much built in since each VM runs its own separate kernel and operating system.

00:06:27.910 --> 00:06:36.360
And then number 3 is legacy, and what I mean by that, is that VMs are well-suited for running legacy applications

00:06:36.360 --> 00:06:44.139
that rely on specific operating system versions or configurations that might not be compatible with the host OS or the other containers.

00:06:44.380 --> 00:06:49.330
So you can set up an environment that is perfect for that legacy application and not have to change it.

00:06:50.170 --> 00:06:52.080
Now, what about Docker containers?

00:06:52.090 --> 00:06:55.600
Let's think about some use cases for these.

00:06:56.320 --> 00:07:01.843
And first and foremost, top of the list, is microservices.

00:07:02.340 --> 00:07:08.472
This is probably the most common use for containers-- that Docker containers lightweight nature,

00:07:08.472 --> 00:07:16.410
its fast start up times and it's the ability to package and distribute dependencies, make it ideal for managing microservices-based applications.

00:07:17.580 --> 00:07:20.609
Number 2, it's just the speed of things.

00:07:20.610 --> 00:07:32.665
So the rapid development and deployment in the ability to use the Docker containers because we can quickly build, deploy and scale up these containers

00:07:32.665 --> 00:07:40.938
and that makes them well-suited for agile development practices and continuous integration/continuous deployment, or CI/CD pipelines.

00:07:41.310 --> 00:07:46.959
And then 3, on a similar theme, is resource efficiency.

00:07:48.030 --> 00:07:54.078
Which is just to say that containers share the same host kernel and have a much smaller footprint than VMs,

00:07:54.078 --> 00:08:00.540
and that allows more containers to run on the same piece of hardware with less overhead.

00:08:00.960 --> 00:08:09.809
So, look, in selecting between Docker and VMs, essentially you'll need to consider the specific needs of your applications and infrastructure.

00:08:09.810 --> 00:08:18.996
And it's common to see both technologies used in hybrid environments where legacy applications might run on VMs

00:08:18.996 --> 00:08:25.470
and then modern microservices-based applications, they'll probably use Docker containers.

00:08:25.860 --> 00:08:29.790
It's really not an either or choice for organizations.

00:08:30.090 --> 00:08:31.649
Commonly, you'll see both.

00:08:31.650 --> 00:08:40.019
And either way, these virtualization technologies have transformed the way we deploy and manage applications.

